
    Microservices – Day 2 Task

--> Service Discovery
        Why Service Discovery is needed
        In microservices:
        Services scale up/down dynamically
        IP addresses & ports change frequently
        Hard-coding URLs like http://localhost:8082 ❌ is not reliable
        👉 Service Discovery solves this by allowing services to find each other dynamically using service names.

--> Client-side vs Server-side Discovery
        | Type                      | How it works                                          | Example             |
        | ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
        | **Client-side discovery** | Client asks registry & decides which instance to call | **Eureka**          |
        | **Server-side discovery** | Load balancer decides target service                  | Kubernetes, AWS ELB |

--> Netflix Eureka Overview
        Eureka is a service registry
        Microservices register themselves
        Other services discover them by name
        Key components:
        Eureka Server → Registry
        Eureka Client → Microservices

--> Service Registration & Heartbeat
        Each service sends a heartbeat every 30 sec
        If heartbeat stops → service is removed
        Prevents calling dead services

--> Spring Cloud Config Server
        Problems with Local Config Files
        Same config duplicated in every service
        Change requires rebuild & redeploy
        Hard to manage multiple environments

--> Centralized Configuration Concept
        All configs stored in Git
        Services fetch config at startup
        One change → affects all services

--> Refresh Scope & Profiles
        @RefreshScope → refresh config without restart
        Profiles:
        dev
        test
        prod

--> Inter-Service Communication

        RestTemplate vs WebClient vs OpenFeign
        | Feature        | RestTemplate | WebClient    | OpenFeign   |
        | -------------- | ------------ | ------------ | ----------- |
        | Style          | Blocking     | Non-blocking | Declarative |
        | Code           | Boilerplate  | Complex      | Clean       |
        | Load balancing | Manual       | Manual       | Automatic   |
        | Recommended    | ❌ Deprecated | ✔ Reactive   | ✔ Best      |


       Feign Client Basics
        Interface-based REST client
        No boilerplate HTTP code
        Works seamlessly with Eureka

      Load Balancing (Eureka + Feign)
       Feign uses service name
       Eureka returns instances
       Calls are auto load-balanced




















